From a6c1e98bafc24550357288ffb8f1b81a20045d65 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: cpkt9762 <cpkt9762@163.com>
Date: Sun, 1 Nov 2026 03:55:33 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] feat(cloud): opt-in automatic project grants for managed
 identities

A managed identity (a device token minted through the admin API) is
authorized per project against cloud_project_grants, so every project a
device enrolls had to be granted by hand before its first push, and the
client retried the 403 every minute until someone did. The shared legacy
token, by contrast, is checked against ENGRAM_CLOUD_ALLOWED_PROJECTS only.

Add ENGRAM_CLOUD_AUTO_GRANT (off by default) and ENGRAM_CLOUD_AUTO_GRANT_MAX
(default 200, counts manual grants too; an invalid value stops startup even
when the switch is off). When on, a managed identity that touches an
ungranted project through any project-scoped route gets the grant created
for itself:

- Only a typed "not granted" answer triggers it. Any other authorizer error,
  an empty name, "*", the reserved inbox name, a disabled identity and a full
  quota are all denied.
- One READ COMMITTED transaction locks the identity row, rereads its grants,
  checks the quota and inserts one grant.auto_create audit row plus the
  grant for each missing project, so a failed audit leaves no grant. Racing
  first requests yield one grant and one audit row; racing distinct
  projects cannot exceed the quota.
- A prompt-pair claim authorizes both projects together, all or nothing.
- Denials keep the exact 403 body clients parse. With the switch off the
  denial path logs nothing new; with it on, decision reasons are logged at
  most once per reason, identity and project per minute.
- Aggregate mutation pulls without a project are unchanged, and the legacy
  token path is unchanged (an allowlist of "*" already allows every project).

Granting is per identity and persists across tokens and switch-off; DOCS.md
spells out that revoking a grant only sticks while the switch is off, and
that a stolen device token can write and delete in any project it names.
Verified on a throwaway database inside the production Postgres: parallel
first requests, quota races, disabled identities, the legacy token, and a
403 body byte-identical to the previous binary.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
