+Automatic managed project grants are **off by default**. Set `ENGRAM_CLOUD_AUTO_GRANT=1` (also accepts `true`, `yes`, or `on`, case-insensitive) to let an authenticated managed identity create its own project grant on its first explicitly scoped sync read or write. The server then stops reviewing which project names that identity may access; local “join sync” enrollment determines ordinary client uploads, but a token holder can request other project names directly. In particular, enabling this also grants read access to encrypted cloud data under any requested project name, subject to the limit. Data remains field-level encrypted; granting access does not supply decryption keys. A stolen ordinary device token can also submit schema-valid writes and deletion changes to an automatically granted project, and create bindings and claims when needed; field-level encryption does not protect these operations. Automatic grants are persistent identity-level permissions shared by all tokens of that identity: turning the switch off, revoking an individual token, or rotating an individual token does not revoke existing grants. Keep the switch off if project membership requires server approval.
+
+`ENGRAM_CLOUD_AUTO_GRANT_MAX` defaults to `200` and bounds the identity's current total grant count when adding automatic grants, including manually issued grants. Existing grants remain usable at or above the limit. Concurrent automatic grant transactions serialize on the identity row lock. The cap reduces project-name abuse; it provides neither a request rate limit nor a cumulative quota, and auto-grant has no waiting timeout of its own. Empty, wildcard (`*`), and reserved inbox project names cannot be automatically granted. Grant names use the existing normalization, so case and punctuation/Unicode variants can share a grant key; this does not rename stored project data. The two project grants required by a prompt-pair claim are created together or neither is created. Aggregate mutation pulls continue filtering by existing grants and do not create new ones. Grant writes and `grant.auto_create` audit events commit in one transaction; storage or audit failure rejects access.
+
+To inspect grants, a managed admin can use `GET /admin/users/{principalID}/grants`. Inspect automatic grant events in the admin audit log at `/dashboard/admin/audit-log` (list partial: `/dashboard/admin/audit-log/list`), matching action `grant.auto_create`; actor and target are the identity itself and metadata includes `{"auto":true}`. Turning auto-grant off stops new automatic grants; it does not revoke existing grants. A manual revocation can be recreated on the next explicit sync access while auto-grant remains enabled. Legacy env-token allowlist requirements and behavior, including `*`, are unchanged.
+
 ### Managed users, tokens, and CLI bootstrap
 
 `engram cloud bootstrap admin` creates the first **managed admin** — a principal record stored in the cloud Postgres database (`cloud_principals` / `cloud_human_users`), independent from the legacy `ENGRAM_CLOUD_TOKEN` / `ENGRAM_CLOUD_ADMIN` env-token model:
@@ -1137,10 +1147,10 @@ This path requires exactly one enabled managed human admin and exactly one activ
 
 - Set `ENGRAM_CLOUD_TOKEN_PEPPER` to enable managed-token authentication. A token issued by `engram cloud bootstrap admin --issue-token` (or by the dashboard/`/admin/*` token-create routes) then authenticates directly against `/sync/*` and `/admin/*`, and can log into the dashboard as its resolved principal/role.
 - If `ENGRAM_CLOUD_TOKEN_PEPPER` is not set, managed-token authentication is simply disabled: the server still starts normally, and `ENGRAM_CLOUD_TOKEN` / `ENGRAM_CLOUD_ADMIN` continue to authenticate exactly as before (legacy-only mode).
-- Managed principals are deny-by-default for project sync: a managed token only reaches projects explicitly granted via `--grant-project` (or the dashboard/`/admin/*` grant routes). Legacy `ENGRAM_CLOUD_TOKEN` keeps its existing `ENGRAM_CLOUD_ALLOWED_PROJECTS` allowlist behavior, unaffected by managed grants.
+- Managed principals are deny-by-default for project sync unless `ENGRAM_CLOUD_AUTO_GRANT` is enabled: by default a managed token only reaches projects explicitly granted via `--grant-project` (or the dashboard/`/admin/*` grant routes). Legacy `ENGRAM_CLOUD_TOKEN` keeps its existing `ENGRAM_CLOUD_ALLOWED_PROJECTS` allowlist behavior, unaffected by managed grants or auto-grant.
 - Managed dashboard inventory, details, statistics, browser views, and project sync controls use the principal's grants, not `ENGRAM_CLOUD_ALLOWED_PROJECTS`. Zero grants expose no projects; wildcard access must be explicitly granted. Legacy dashboard credentials remain env-allowlist restricted.
 - Explicit `cloud_project_controls` rows register projects, including empty projects shown with zero counts. Existing projects with synced content remain visible for compatibility, subject to the same credential scope. Updating project sync controls refreshes dashboard inventory.
-- Disabled managed users, revoked managed tokens, and revoked project grants stop authenticating/authorizing on the very next request — no server restart required.
+- Disabled managed users and revoked managed tokens stop authenticating on the very next request — no server restart required. Revoked project grants stop authorizing on the next request when auto-grant is off. With auto-grant enabled, the same identity can recreate a revoked grant on its next explicit sync access to that project; to make project revocation effective, first turn auto-grant off or disable the identity.
 - No rollback action is required to keep using legacy credentials: legacy `ENGRAM_CLOUD_TOKEN` continues to use its existing sync allowlist, and `ENGRAM_CLOUD_ADMIN` continues to provide dashboard read access, project sync controls, audit logs, and the first-admin dashboard bootstrap entry point whether or not `ENGRAM_CLOUD_TOKEN_PEPPER` is configured. The CLI recovery command remains limited to its documented stranded-admin state. Use a managed admin token for managed-user administration.
 
